Pretty disappointing, I must say. It's not a little dated, it's
antediluvian. Also, it teaches some bad habits.
- It teaches K&R style function declaration. This _is_ the style used in
NetHack, but it's not a style you should be learning as a beginner these
days, and hasn't been for almost 20 years. It mentions prototypes in
passing, but doesn't explain anything about them. You should start off
declaring your functions with prototypes, and learn K&R style
declarations as an addendum.
- Its handling of malloc() is also prehistoric. You shouldn't declare
it, or any other Standard C function, yourself (and certainly not
wrongly, as it does), but include the Standard header that declares it;
and casting malloc() calls is a bad idea in C (unfortunately it's
required, for some pretty half-baked reasons, in C++; but you shouldn't
use malloc() in C++ anyway, you should use new).
- There is a lithmus test for C tutorials and books: if they mention
gets() without telling you not to use it, and without telling you how to
use fgets() instead, ditch it _now_. This one passes that only halfway;
it suggests that gets() _may_ be dangerous, while the way it is defined
in the Standard means that it _must_ be except in rather unusual
compilers.
- It assumes a Unix-style compiler and some Unix-style extensions.
I could go on, but let's not.

Two web tutorials on C which I haven't seen myself, but which come
recommended by people who know what they're talking about, are
<http://cprog.tomsweb.net/> and
<http://www.eskimo.com/~scs/cclass/cclass.html>.

But you could do a lot worse than buy Kernighan and Ritchie's "The C
Programming Language", Second Edition. You almost certainly won't be
able to buy the First Edition, but you wouldn't want to anyway, unless
you are already a C programmer and enthusiast (personally, I'd love
one), since it describes only K&R C, which as mentioned above is now out
of date, and not ISO Standard C. K&R2 is the best introduction to C I
know (not too surprising, since Dennis Ritchie designed the language),
and a good introduction to programming in general for the already
computer-literate.

Some other information you could use: the comp.lang.c FAQ, at
<http://www.c-faq.com/>; and the ISO C Standard, which does not make
good bedtime reading unless you're already a C expert, but does tell you
exactly what is in Standard C, how it is officially defined, and often
equally important, what is _not_ defined (such as, e.g., the order in
which function parameters are passed). The official Standard itself is
not free, but this <www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n1256.pdf>
is the working document of the Standard Committee (which defines it),
and consists of the original official 1999 version of the ISO C
Standard, with Technical Corrigenda 1 and 2, and the latest draft of TC
3, added in. The differences between it and the current Standard are
slight (or possibly non-existent), since they should consist only of the
final changes to the 3rd Technical Corrigendum, which itself is not a
massive emendation (more of an elucidation) of the Standard itself.

